64ef" "q-131256/selection/b62cf32b8a413caeb2fc38e23bccca503f531fd59c8157b262d407f3c76e82a5" "q-131256/selection/3e81f8508c4a799c59586058a4bc8640a1298b91dab0c2e3054d29c9152e4e95" "q-131256/selection/ed2f34668e55b312d1b7beddcf79967f1f147e691ed5e7697f9bb4a34216dcbe" "q-131256/selection/75eb24a17cb87711b88ed552441e72c301cc54facc0fed1b0cc9893a66070ea5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131256" :question/id #uuid "bcd84b01-ed2a-54d5-a814-fd8e2737e1eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-131256" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{34}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{34}=3$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/a